Subject: The God of Hacking

I find it amusing that Eric Raymond doesn't seem to be aware of any God of
Hacking.  Hermes would seem to qualify.  He did, after all, invent the Lyre
of Appollo, among other things.  I've always found a strong association in
mythologies of tricksters, discoverers, and inventors.  Prometheus and the
analogous fire-stealers would also seem to qualify, but Hermes remains my
favorite: messenger, patron God of Thieves, inventor - sounds lke a Hacker
to me!

But, then again, my impression of "Hacking" seems different than the way
the term is sometimes used by those older than I.  Based upon my reading
of "Generations: The History of America's Future" (which I'm going to get
around to writing a review of after I post the lyrics to "Tomorrow's World"
by Joe Jackson :-), I've been toying with describing the generation right
after the Baby Boomers as the "Hacker Generation."  Comments?  It seems to
fit better than "Thirteeners," or, as Wired likes to call it, "The Digital
Generation."

It's also amusing that Ray Cromwell's taking umbrage (if that is the right
word) at alleged egotism on Eric Raymond's part.  If so, then he's merely
revealing his own weakness.  If he really is trying to dominate the group,
then egotism will only hurt his effort.  And pointing it out to him will
only help it.

