Suppose you have three atoms.  Two atoms of argon and an
atom of Xenon. Nice, simple van der Waals forces, no
resonance structures.

You have a table giving the exact forces between a pair of
Argon atoms. A simple function of distance.  Similarly you
have a table giving the force between an Argon atom and
Xenon atom.

Does this mean that you can predict the forces in a three
atom cluster, a cluster of two argon atoms and a Xenon atom
by simply adding up the forces between pairs of atoms? 
Nope.  Orders of magnitude harder problem. If you simply
add up the pairwise forces you will get an answer that is
not merely wrong, but drastically wrong.

The presence of one atom alters the force between the other
two atoms.

