In <9307070740.AA05592@geech.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)  wrote:

>   On the subject of smoothly sliding nanosurfaces, I remember reading
> a short blurb about "nano-sandblasting" used by STM researchers (written about
> a year ago).  They used their technique to polish nanosurfaces in preparation
> for nano-grafitti and noted that atoms they tried to lay down on the surface
> rolled around quite smoothly until they doped the surface. 

Air molecules rattle around with no problem.  Large
molecules tend to stay put.  When you get really small, kT
becomes large compared to binding energy.

>   I would like to see someone in this group actually present some calculations
> rather than just waving their hands. 

No can do.  The maths of van der Waals forces, done
properly, is truly awesome and is not for the faint
hearted, but we can do something better.  We can look at
actual systems, thanks to the miracle of scanning tunneling
electron microscopes.

Consider one carbon nanotube inside another.  The two
tubes, having different radii will necessarily have
different helicities.  Therefore, by Drexler's method of
calculations, their surrounding "force fields" will have
different spatial frequencies, and will cancel out, so that
the inner tube will be able to slide freely inside the
outer tube.

We can "see" the moiri pattern made by the inner tube
against the outer tube.  Where the two lattices line up,
the pi orbitals on the two tubes alter, and the tube
develops a bump - the electrons extend further out from
the nuclei. (Recent copy of "Science" - I do not remember
the date, sometime in the last few months - I assume I am
not the only person on this list who gets "Science"

Suppose that the inner tube were to move a small distance.
A small movement in the inner tube would result in a large
shift in the moiri pattern. (Vernier effect.)

That moiri pattern is not a mere optical effect - since
the electron clouds are visibly different, further
extended, the van Der Waals forces should be significantly
different.  Now if the inner tube was to move a small
amount, the moiri pattern would have to move a large
amount.  It would bump into things around the tube, like
the graphite surface supporting the tube, and cause the
outer tube to lurch around.

Small movement would produce a redistribution of force
with low spatial frequency.

> It reminds me of the arguments
> against Single Stage rocket technology which almost never present numbers.
> I'd like to see a comparison of the sticking forces involved vs the driving
> forces.  I'm don't want insult anyone, but I'm far more inclined to believe 
> that Drexler has done his homework (and actually modelled and computed the 
> average forces involved). 

Drexler used the model that the forces were pairwise
additive independent. Just about everyone uses this model
because it is the only way one can do calculations, not
because it gives realistic results.

My description above of concentric graphite nanotubes is
describing the van Der Waals forces as if they were triplet
wise additive independent, which is almost as inaccurate,
though it does describe the Z shaped bump that shows up in
the scanning tunneling electron micrographs, whereas
pairwise independence does not.


