Martyn J. Fogg. <mfogg@cix.compulink.co.uk> quotes "the 
Russian scientist Vladimir Vernadsky (1863-1945)":
 
> "...There arises the problem of the reconstruction of the 
> biosphere in the interests of a freely thinking humanity as a
> single totality. ...  He can and
> must rebuild the province of his life by his work and thought,
> rebuild it radically in comparision with the past. Wider and
> wider creative possibilities open before him."

I don't know if Mr. Fogg, or others, realize that this is 
the standard Marxist way of thinking about things.  Mankind
consciously, rationally, freely, taking evolution into its 
hands "as a single totality".  (Totality is a Hegelian/Marxist 
keyword.)

> I suspect this would give most green lefties apoplexy!...
> ...Vernadsky was, in fact, the very epitome of an Extropian.

Of course Marxists had and have different ideas from ours about 
what's rational and free.  But is the distinction obvious?

I think that Extropianism, superficially stated, segues quite 
smoothly into Marxism.  Rational transformation as a philosopy 
has already been done.  In fact, the association with Marxism 
(and capital-O Objectivism, another brand of gung-ho 
"rationalism") probably makes people sensibly wary.  The 
distinguishing features of Extropianism must be, or had better 
be, more subtle.  I think if those things could be crystalized 
and stuck in the front, so to speak, of the presentation of 
Extropianism, it might have both better appeal and a clearer 
self-image.

